Here are our all binary operators, from highest to lowest precedence1:
    - Multiplication & division (*, /, %)
    - Addition & subtraction (+,-)
    - Shift left (<<) & right (>>)
    - Relational lt/gt/lte/gte (<, >,<=,>=)
    - Relational equal/not equal (==, !=)
    - Bitwise AND (&)
    - Bitwise XOR (^)
    - Bitwise OR (|)
    - Logical AND (&&)
    - Logical OR (||)


 - importantly, where and when ought a unary op be applied
 - on only an int, an applied unary op, an exp in "()"
 - so then the def. of an exp becomes a binary op applied exp or a unary op applicable exp i.e., <factor>
 - we don't let a unary operator compete with a binary op
 - so we simply take a unary operator and attach it to the very next unary applicable expression
 - if we are to apply a unary operator to a binary exp, it must already be bracketed, evaluated as an indie exp i.e., <int> or, be a unary applied expression
 - which is basically where unary op are already applicable

 - we can make a similar change to force "*" and "/" to be higher precedence than "+" and "-"
 - so now a "*" or "/" applicable expression can be something which already has a unary operator attached to it, if there is one to be attached
 - thus, a <term> can be a unary applicable expression or, a "*" or "/" applied expression

 - now "+" and "-" can be applied to an already "*" or "/" applied expression, or a "+" and "-" applicable expression
 - and that is the lowest precedence we can go so a "+" and "-" applicable expression is simply the <exp>

 - now we need to handle associativity which is to say we need to make stuff left associative
 - so that we can compute the expressions by looking ahead finitely many steps
 - just 1 step ahead in our case(best case) which is the LL(1) grammar
 - for left ass. we take an evaluated expression and look that we a "+" or "-"
 - so now we take the smallest "+" and "-" applicable expression i.e., <term>
 - and bundle it with that sign and shove it the LHS expression

 - but that wud make the grammar left recursive since when we try to parse an <exp>,
 - the LHS exp is an <exp> type which is again parsed and so on..., can't be handled by recursive descent
 - we just flip the <exp> and <term> positions so now we get right recursive grammar
 - similar sorcery for higher precedence applicable expression types

 - but alas this becomes right associative again!
 - understand that in any case we must begin a "+" and "-" applicable expression with the smallest of the kind i.e., <term>
 - so we keep the LHS side as it is, and understand the RHS <exp> in terms of its constituent <term>s
 - which is where we get an evaluate <exp> by adding two <term>s which in now ready to get another <term> to be added to it
 - similar sorcery for higher precedence applicable expression types

 - and mind you it is associativity that takes care of "precedence" when same type of ops are in conflict



 - Oooh, we have CONDITIONALS now, OMG!
 - e1 ? e2 : e3
 - this op has the least precedence of all then we have somewhat of such order
 - exp, assignment, conditional, logicals...

 - e1 can't be an assignment exp bcs "=" has lower precedence than "?"
 - and it can't be conditional lest we fall over a slope, so it is a logical-or-exp
 - e2 can be any general exp since ? and : are implicit parentheses
 - e3 can be any general exp except it can't be an assignment exp
 - because of left associativity of ":", we get smthg like (.. ? .. :) = ..
 - which is erroneous as ?: is not assignable
 - so e3 is a conditional exp

 - since we are defining all logicals as a conditional, we make the ?.. and following part optional








